        "title": "04 - Philosophy of Mind",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nSUBSTANCE DUALISM (Descartes):\n  Two kinds of substance: res cogitans (thinking stuff, mind) + res extensa (extended stuff, body)\n  Mind is not spatial; body is spatial; they causally interact (pineal gland, per Descartes)\n  INTERACTION PROBLEM: if they're fundamentally different substances, how do they causally interact?\n    Physical causal closure: every physical effect has a sufficient physical cause\n    If true: mental causation either requires overdetermination (weird) or mind is physical\n  EPIPHENOMENALISM: mental events are caused by physical but don't cause anything\n    Mental events are like steam from a train: causally inert byproducts of brain activity\n    Problem: seems to make mental reasoning causally irrelevant; your beliefs about arguments\n      don't affect your body's actions — just brain states do"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md#009",
      "path": [
        "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind, git-history:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, mind]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-mind",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "04 - Philosophy of Mind",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "PROPERTY DUALISM:\n  Only one substance (physical); but mental properties are fundamentally distinct from physical\n  Non-reductive physicalism: mental properties supervene on physical but aren't reducible to them\n  Chalmers: consciousness involves phenomenal properties that \"dangle\" ontologically\n  SUPERVENIENCE: no mental difference without physical difference\n    Strong supervenience: necessarily, same physical → same mental (same complete physical state\n      means same mental state in every possible world)\n    But supervenience doesn't explain WHY physical gives rise to mental"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md#010",
      "path": [
        "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind, git-history:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, mind]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-mind",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "04 - Philosophy of Mind",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "TYPE IDENTITY THEORY (Place, Smart):\n  Mental state types = physical state types\n  \"Pain\" = \"C-fibers firing\" (or whatever the relevant neural state turns out to be)\n  Empirical thesis: discover the identity through neuroscience\n  PROBLEM: multiple realizability — pain in humans, octopuses, silicon beings all count\n    as pain even though they realize it in different physical substrates → type identity too narrow"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md#011",
      "path": [
        "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind, git-history:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, mind]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-mind",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "04 - Philosophy of Mind",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "ELIMINATIVE MATERIALISM (Paul and Patricia Churchland):\n  Folk psychology — the everyday framework of beliefs, desires, intentions,\n    qualia — is a radically false theory, like phlogiston or caloric.\n  Not: mental states are identical to brain states (reductionism).\n  Instead: mental states do not exist at all as described by folk psychology.\n    When neuroscience matures, we will not reduce \"beliefs\" to neural states;\n    we will eliminate the concept entirely, replacing it with a better theory\n    at the neural level.\n  Analogy: we did not reduce \"caloric\" to molecular motion; we eliminated\n    caloric and replaced it with a different vocabulary (kinetic energy).\n  EVIDENCE: Folk psychology doesn't explain sleep, mental illness, creative\n    insight, or anything it claims to explain in mechanistic terms.\n    Its track record of successful prediction is poor.\n  MAIN OBJECTION: The theory seems self-refuting — to assert \"beliefs don't\n    exist\" requires asserting a belief. Paul Churchland: this is just the\n    old framework resisting elimination; the new vocabulary will dissolve\n    the puzzle.\n  PRACTICAL RELEVANCE: Eliminative materialism predicts that \"explainable\n    AI\" in folk-psychological terms (the model \"believes X\" or \"understands Y\")\n    is the wrong framing entirely. The right framing is the computational-\n    neuroscientific one: activation patterns, attention weights, circuit-level\n    mechanisms. Folk-psychological XAI is projecting a false ontology onto\n    the system."
    },
    {
      "id": "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md#012",
      "path": [
        "04-PHILOSOPHY-OF-MIND.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind, git-history:philosophy:04-philosophy-of-mind]",
